Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:24.070 align:start position:0%
 
so  the second generation of the panamera  has been hitting the headlines as  porsche's first plug-in hybrid  which can be charged using a household  electrical socket  the panamera sc hybrid's battery stores  five times as much power as its  predecessor  and at 70 kilowatts its electric motor  is more than twice as powerful  our reviewer says the panamera e hybrid  is quiet as a mouse  all you hear is the tires rolling and  the wind blowing the car has a range of  36 kilometers in pure electric mode  though if driven prudently it can travel  more than 50.  feinholt says that's a quantum leap  compared to its predecessor which  manages two kilometers in electric mode  even when the battery is run down the  panamera doesn't have to be plugged into  a socket  pressing the e charge button turns the  electric motor into a generator  then the gasoline engine drives not only  the wheels its energy is also used to  produce electricity  long drives on the highway can charge  the battery for short emissions-free  jaunts around the city  feinhold is impressed by the way the  e-hybrid switches almost  imperceptibly between gas and electric  mode  the boost function packs the 95  horsepower of the electric motor  onto that of the combustion engine this  makes for 416 horsepower all together  allowing the car to rocket from zero to  100 kilometers an hour  and 5.5 seconds and hit a top speed of  270.  the new hybrid model delivers 306  kilowatts of power  and generates a hefty 590 newton meters  of torque  but thanks to the added electric motor  fuel consumption is just 3.1 liters per  100 kilometers  in electric mode the se hybrid can reach  135 kilometers an hour  not everyone likes the panamera's design  but those who do  like it a lot the rest tend to opt for a  classic 911.  under the hood is a 3 liter v6 engine  that works in tandem with the electric  motor  striking lettering and the porsche  emblem on the wheels  round off the exterior  the battery can be recharged at home  thanks to some special equipment  in germany regular household electrical  sockets have between 8 and 10 amps of  current  explains porsche's robot so it takes  around four hours to charge  the battery fully but if you have access  to high voltage current  you can use that too then a full charge  takes two to three hours  the back end of the e-hybrid is also a  sight to behold  and features led tail lights the trunk  can be opened by pressing a button on  the rear windshield wiper  it offers 335 liters of cargo space  or 1153 liters with the rear seat bench  down  like the panamera gts the se hybrid  uses porsche's active suspension  management system or pasm  it continuously regulates the damping  force for each wheel according to the  road conditions  inside the hybrid looks just like the  regular panamera  say for one tiny detail  the writing on the rev counter  the s e hybrid is fitted with an eight  gear tiptronic s  automatic transmission the leather seats  are comfortable and ooze quality  workmanship  the ultimate in luxury  clearly the se hybrid portion isn't an  eco-friendly car for the masses  its high price tag means it's out of  reach for most  the sedan can be had for 110 000 euros  in germany  but only if you resist the many tempting  optional extras  the smallest member of the alfa romeo  family has had a little surgery  the mito has a new grill that makes the  front of the super mini more streamlined  it's available with five different  gasoline engines and two diesel units  producing between 51 and 125 kilowatts  there's also the mito impression edition  with a new equipment package  prices for the entry level model start  at 14  100 euros  the latest technologies were on show at  the 11th bleh  expo metal work trade fair last week  with a thousand manufacturers gathering  in stuttgart  among the star exhibits were hybrid  materials made from steel  and synthetics that are pushing  lightweight construction forward  and making cars more efficient dual  phase steel is setting new standards for  client seat and strength and opening up  new possibilities for  on-road safety and security  the new volkswagen cross caddy cuts an  imposing figure  in the metallic green tint  the black under right protection and  body panels give the vehicle a robust  appearance  and a hint of adventure  our testament says the caddy and the  cross caddy are both utility vehicles  but many young families opt to buy one  because they're incredibly practical  with rear sliding doors and immense  storage space  mata says if you ignore the plastic on  the dashboard  which looks like a typical utility  vehicle the cross caddy runs like  any premium passenger car  we're testing the cross caddy with a two  liter diesel engine  and four wheel drive in germany the  basic model of this version  costs at least thirty three thousand  five hundred euros  extras can take that up to over forty  thousand  our cross caddy has a hill start assist  function  says the cross caddy has an advantage  over the other volkswagen cross models  because it doesn't just look like an  off-roader it has four-wheel drive too  that means it's fit for all kinds of use  whether by workmen  or families  as is typical for a caddy the trunk is  huge  and it has the usual high ride height  that ground clearance means the cross  caddy has an easy ride  even on unpaved roads but it's also a  benefit on paved once  as it gives the driver a better overview  and traffic  the cockpit is clear and functional like  on other vw models  the green exterior spills over to the  inside  although buyers have a choice between  green and gray  we think this combination has more kick  to it  there are more storage spaces all over  the car too  the caddy was designed as a utility  vehicle but became popular among  families  now volkswagen is taking aim at that  market  so does the cross give buyers something  extra  says that at 40 000 euros the cross  caddy we're driving isn't cheap  but for that you get a vehicle with  loads of room that's well made  and will hold its value  kemia is a beautiful seaside resort in  southwest turkey normally a place to  relax and rejuvenate  for one weekend a year however that  peace and calm is disrupted when the  world's best off-road riders gather here  for the sea to sky event  they start from sea level literally by  the sea before scaling  mount olympus at an altitude of 2365  meters  it's always a good atmosphere you know  like the tourists come they've probably  never seen anything like this so there's  a few heads turning  looking what we're doing so it's  introducing people to the sport as well  and something they've never seen  the writers start day one on the beach  but hardly as bums  this opening stage of the race is all  about speed  thrills spills and spectacular jumps  21 year old johnny walker leaves his  rivals in the dust during qualifying  grabbing pole position for the next  stage  i'm i'm feeling good but it's like every  other race i've been to this year i've  been feeling good but  like herzberg i drowned my bike in the  first water puddle and then  romania because i ran out of fuel so  hopefully nothing goes wrong with this  one  then the battle against the elements  commences from the beach  they head for the forest as the mountain  begins to loom larger  the terrain only gets tougher from here  onwards and the riders will have to use  all their skills to prevail  smooth and swift sections are followed  by tricky technical parts  pushing man and machine to their limits  it's the kind of conditions that  two-time winner graham jarvis revels in  and he makes up ground on fellow  englishman walker  so if a win this year it'll be three  wins on the trot  so would be a nice i'm still hungry as  ever  still really want that win as the race  progresses it becomes increasingly a  quest for climbers  rather than cruisers on this kind of  terrain  experience and technical riding skills  are decisive  not horsepower even the best of them  struggle to overcome  seemingly impassable obstacles wielding  those heavy  100 kilo bikes around corner after  corner  is visibly taking its toll that's an  immense  physical exertion and not just because  of the terrain  the hardest part of this race i think  it's the heat we have  30 more than 30 degrees and yeah  that's the hardest part for me and also  in the  up at the mountain i heard about the  dark canyon  the dark canyon is the final grueling  stage of the sea to sky  only the clem de la creme make it this  far  the exhausted riders have to summon up  whatever strength  they have left to conquer the summit  it's been a neck and neck race between  walker and jarvis  and there's just a few meters separating  them as the finish line comes into view  walker wins ending his streak of bad  luck  the 10 second winning margin was one of  the closest ever an extreme enduro  i think the hardest part for me was  probably coming up towards the end when  uh  when i seen jarvis was catching me i  knew he was coming all all day so  i just i just had to push and then the  last four miles maybe we were both  going backwards and forwards in  positions and eventually got 10 seconds  on him at the top and  that's all that split on his road really  well so  big congratulations to him in third  no small achievement here is an  exhausted alfredo gomez from spain  for dougie lampkin it seemed the torment  would never end  basically i could see the i could see  the red bull arch here  and i thought it was getting close but i  knew the bike was just coming to a stop  and then it looked like it was on fire  there was blue smoke coming from under  the tank and  unfortunately somebody was throwing  water on it just on that corner and then  the bike just stopped then  and i knew there was no chance trying to  restart it i knew that was the end  and one of the photographers put his  cameras down and said let's push it and  i thought  luckily i got some more friends to come  down and give us a hand and  johnny came down now you know after  winning the race came down and got me  through to the end  extreme enduro is the ultimate in  off-road biking  but there's also a lot of camaraderie  between competitors  only the three guys on the podium get  trophies but  every rider who finishes the race is  surely a winner  santa gadda bolognese italy one of those  villages where time seems to have stood  still  everyone knows everyone and everyone  knows lamborghini  this beauty goes by the name job a  survivor of the company's era in  receivership in the early 1980s  and itself marking the end of a  different kind of era  the jaapa was the last lamborghini to be  powered by a v8 engine  the jolpa's roof can be swiftly stowed  away behind the seats  it was lamborghini's answer to ferrari's  popular 308 gts  the company museum on the via modina  houses a choice selection of the luxury  marquis models  lamborghini the name alone is music to  the ears of sports car fans  it took founder ferruccio lamborghini  little over a decade to make his marquee  legend thanks to the 400 gt  the mura and the futuristic [ __ ] touch  1970 had marked the unveiling of the  araco with an all-new clean sheet design  and an eight-cylinder overhead cam  engine the araco  didn't actually hit the market until  1973 however  787 of the first version were built  during its production run through the  end of the decade  its successor the lamborghini silhouette  came in a very limited edition  just 52.  the series culminated in the job 420 of  them were produced from 1982 to 88  lamborghini's last v8 sports car  the 255 horsepower propelled the java  from zero to 100 kilometers per hour in  6.9 seconds  reaching a maximum speed of 240 one of  the fastest cars around  in the early 1980s  but who cares about the bear stats when  you can indulge in the growl of the  8 cylinder beast under the bonnet  the classic toggle switches were not to  be found in the job  the driver monitors engine performance  and speed on dials arranged  in a practical square cube by 1982  standards air conditioning and fine  leather upholstery  made the interior practically palatial  the chaplain may not be a classical  beauty but its design is  uncompromising and shamelessly italian  the exterior boosts flared fenders  and a wedge-shaped body  the mirror proportions of the car  suggest where the v8 is located  behind the driver experienced drivers  will also feel the rear mid engine in  the car's handling  this car is far more agile than the  jalpa's competitors  testing the car's limits is best left up  to the experts  the rear mid engine can be a tricky  monster to master  in 1982 you could get a jopla for about  the same price as a porsche 911  convertible  and a vw golf gti together  today ajapa in sound condition still has  a price tag of some 50 000 euros  the jumper was the last of the  lamborghini v8s  and is for many people the  quintessential italian sports car  of the 1980s  you

